steps.body:
A TypeScript class declares each field in its body, with a type. The constructor takes typed parameters and assigns the fields:

```typescript
class Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;

  constructor(x: number, y: number) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }
}

const p = new Point(3, 4);
console.log(p.x + p.y); // 7
```

The class name is also a type, the type of its instances, so you can write `const origin: Point` or a parameter `(p: Point)`.

Under `strict`, every field must get a value, either from an initializer where it's declared (`count = 0`) or from the constructor. A field that gets neither is TS2564, because reading it would give `undefined` where its type promises something else. A field with an initializer infers its type from it, the way a `let` variable does.

`readonly` lets you set a field only in its declaration or in the constructor. Assigning it anywhere else is TS2540.

examples.code:
class BankAccount {
  owner: string;
  balance: number;

  constructor(owner: string, balance: number) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.balance = balance;
  }

  deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
  }

  withdraw(amount: number): boolean {
    if (amount > this.balance) {
      return false;
    }
    this.balance -= amount;
    return true;
  }
}

const account = new BankAccount("Ada", 100);
//    ^? const account: BankAccount
account.deposit(50);
console.log(`Withdraw 200: ${account.withdraw(200)}`);
console.log(`Withdraw 120: ${account.withdraw(120)}`);
console.log(`${account.owner} has $${account.balance} left`);

class Ticket {
  title: string;
  status: string;

  constructor(title: string) {
    this.title = title;
  }
}

const ticket = new Ticket("Fix login");
console.log(`${ticket.title}: ${ticket.status.toUpperCase()}`);

examples.explanation:
The constructor never sets `status`, so a new ticket would hold `undefined` there while its type says `string`, and `status.toUpperCase()` would crash. `tsc` reports the field at its declaration instead, before anything runs. Give it an initializer, or assign it in the constructor:

```typescript
class Ticket {
  title: string;
  status = "open";

  constructor(title: string) {
    this.title = title;
  }
}

const ticket = new Ticket("Fix login");
console.log(`${ticket.title}: ${ticket.status.toUpperCase()}`); // Fix login: OPEN
```

The initializer runs for every new ticket, before the constructor's body. When a field really can be empty, say so in its type and start it there, like `assignee: string | null = null`.

examples.code:
class Ticket {
  readonly id: string;
  title: string;
  status = "open";
  //^? (property) Ticket.status: string
  comments: string[] = [];

  constructor(id: string, title: string) {
    this.id = id;
    this.title = title;
  }

  close(note: string): void {
    this.comments.push(note);
    this.status = "closed";
  }
}

const ticket = new Ticket("T-101", "Fix login");
ticket.close("Fixed in 2.4.1");
console.log(`${ticket.id} ${ticket.title}: ${ticket.status}`);
console.log(`Comments: ${ticket.comments.join(", ")}`);

examples.explanation:
`readonly id` is assigned once, in the constructor, and never changes after that. `status` and `comments` start from initializers, so the constructor doesn't mention them. The bubble shows that `status` is a `string`, not `"open"`. A field can change, so its literal type widens the way a `let` does, which is why `close()` may set it to `"closed"`. To allow only some strings, annotate it: `status: "open" | "closed" = "open"`. `comments` needs its annotation for another reason: a bare `[]` would be a `never[]` that nothing can be pushed into.

Assigning the id after the constructor is TS2540:

```typescript error
class Ticket {
  readonly id: string;

  constructor(id: string) {
    this.id = id;
  }
}

const ticket = new Ticket("T-101");
ticket.id = "T-102";
console.log(ticket.id);
```

