steps.body:
TypeScript has two ways to make a field private. The `private` modifier is a TypeScript rule: the checker rejects any use outside the class, then Node erases it, so at runtime the field is an ordinary property. A `#` field is JavaScript's own private field, from the JavaScript course. The runtime enforces it, and it never shows up in `Object.keys` or `JSON.stringify`.

```typescript
class Wallet {
  private pin = "1234";
  #balance = 50;
}

const wallet = new Wallet();
console.log(Object.keys(wallet).join(", ")); // pin
console.log(wallet["pin"]); // 1234
```

Outside the class, `wallet.pin` and `wallet.#balance` are both type errors. But only the checker guards `private`, so code that gets around it still reaches the value. Even TypeScript lets a bracket lookup like `wallet["pin"]` through.

`protected` works like `private`, except that subclasses may use it too. Outside code still can't. `public` is the default, so you rarely write it.

examples.code:
class Account {
  owner: string;
  private pin: string;
  #balance: number;

  constructor(owner: string, pin: string, balance: number) {
    this.owner = owner;
    this.pin = pin;
    this.#balance = balance;
  }

  withdraw(pin: string, amount: number): string {
    if (pin !== this.pin) {
      return "Wrong PIN";
    }
    if (amount > this.#balance) {
      return "Not enough money";
    }
    this.#balance -= amount;
    return `Paid ${amount}, ${this.#balance} left`;
  }
}

const account = new Account("Ada", "4321", 100);
console.log(account.withdraw("0000", 20));
console.log(account.withdraw("4321", 20));
console.log(`Keys: ${Object.keys(account).join(", ")}`);
console.log(`JSON: ${JSON.stringify(account)}`);

examples.explanation:
To the checker, `pin` and `#balance` are equally private: `withdraw` uses both, and code outside the class can use neither. At runtime they differ. `private` is erased, so `pin` is an ordinary property. `Object.keys` lists it, and `JSON.stringify` writes the PIN out, where a log line or an API response could leak it. `#balance` lives in a slot that only the class's own code can reach, so neither of them sees it.

Here's what reading them from outside looks like to the checker:

```typescript error
class Account {
  private pin = "4321";
  #balance = 100;
}

const account = new Account();
console.log(account.pin);
console.log(account.#balance);
```

`tsc` reports TS2341 for the first, `Property 'pin' is private and only accessible within class 'Account'.`, and TS18013 for the second, `Property '#balance' is not accessible outside class 'Account' because it has a private identifier.`

examples.code:
class Logger {
  protected label: string;
  lines: string[] = [];

  constructor(label: string) {
    this.label = label;
  }

  info(message: string): void {
    this.lines.push(`[${this.label}] ${message}`);
  }
}

class AuditLogger extends Logger {
  audit(user: string, action: string): void {
    this.lines.push(`[${this.label} audit] ${user} ${action}`);
  }
}

const log = new AuditLogger("orders");
log.info("server started");
log.audit("ada", "refunded ord_42");
console.log(log.lines.join("\n"));

examples.explanation:
`label` is `protected`, so `AuditLogger`, a subclass, reads `this.label` to build its own lines. `AuditLogger` has no constructor of its own, so it uses the one it inherits, and `new AuditLogger("orders")` sets the label. Code outside both classes can't read it:

```typescript error
class Logger {
  protected label: string;

  constructor(label: string) {
    this.label = label;
  }
}

const log = new Logger("orders");
console.log(log.label);
```

That's TS2445: `Property 'label' is protected and only accessible within class 'Logger' and its subclasses.` Use `protected` for what subclasses need and callers don't. Like `private`, only `tsc` checks it, and JavaScript has no runtime version of it.

examples.code:
class ApiClient {
  readonly baseUrl: string;
  #apiKey: string;
  #log: string[] = [];

  constructor(baseUrl: string, apiKey: string) {
    this.baseUrl = baseUrl;
    this.#apiKey = apiKey;
  }

  request(path: string): string {
    const line = `GET ${this.baseUrl}${path} (key ...${this.#apiKey.slice(-4)})`;
    this.#log.push(line);
    return line;
  }

  log(): readonly string[] {
    return this.#log;
  }
}

const client = new ApiClient("https://api.example.com", "sk_test_7f3a9c21");
client.request("/orders");
client.request("/orders/42");
console.log(client.log().join("\n"));
console.log(JSON.stringify(client));

examples.explanation:
Each modifier answers a different question. `readonly baseUrl` is about change: anyone may read it, and nothing can reassign it after the constructor. `#apiKey` is about secrecy. As a `#` field, the runtime keeps it in, so `JSON.stringify`, logging the whole object, or a bracket lookup can't leak it, and the JSON line shows only `baseUrl`. `#log` is internal state that callers may read but not edit, so `log()` returns it typed `readonly string[]`:

```typescript error
class ApiClient {
  #log: string[] = ["GET /orders"];

  log(): readonly string[] {
    return this.#log;
  }
}

const client = new ApiClient();
client.log().push("DELETE /orders");
console.log(client.log().length);
```

That's TS2339, because a `readonly string[]` has no `push`. It's still the same array at runtime, so return a copy, `[...this.#log]`, when code that skips the checker must not change it either. In existing codebases you'll often see `private` where this one uses `#`. It gets the same checks and is fine whenever the checker is the only guard you need. Prefer `#` for anything that must stay hidden at runtime.

